\f0\fs24 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Excellent catch \'97 this is one of the most useful single sentences in the entire Prieve report.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Locating the statement\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The statement appears at 
\f1\b page 6 of the Prieve report (page 9 of the combined 
\f2\fs26 FarmersDenyLetter-11-4.pdf
\f1\fs24 )
\f0\b0 , in the rear/east garage window paragraph:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 "A 2 unit window appears at the rear/east side of the garage adjacent to a shed. Various scrape marks appear in the frame of the window not consistent with hail. 
\f1\b No smooth or rounded dents were observed in the frame or sashes.
\f0\b0 "\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Why it is leverageable \'97 Prieve just defined his own hail signature, then convicted himself\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 In one sentence, Prieve admits the 
\f1\b operative diagnostic test
\f0\b0  he applies to determine whether a dent was caused by hail: a hail dent is "smooth or rounded." He uses the absence of "smooth or rounded dents" at the garage window as the reason to rule out hail there. That same diagnostic \'97 applied to the rest of his own report \'97 defeats his Conclusion 1.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 1. Prieve documents smooth/rounded dents elsewhere \'97 and his own report calls them dents\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 His own narrative records observations that match the very signature he says is hail:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Front/west triple window (A-38\'9641):
\f0\b0  "
\f1\b Rounded dents
\f0\b0  were observed at sashes of this window unit." (Page 4 of the Prieve report / page 7 of the combined PDF.)\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f1\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 North porch four-unit window (A-178\'96179, A-181):
\f0\b0  "Two dents appear at the lower left corner of the left casement 
\f1\b which are generally smooth
\f0\b0 ." (Page 5 of the Prieve report / page 8 of the combined PDF.)\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 So Prieve found smooth or rounded dents \'97 the very thing he uses elsewhere as the marker of hail \'97 at multiple windows on this property. He then handwaves them away by attributing both to "a prior hail claim," not because the dents themselves are different, but because Farmers' 2020 file mentioned those windows. That's not a methodology; it's a presumption.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 2. The "scrape marks inside dents \uc0\u8594  blunt object other than hail" framework is internally inconsistent with his own diagnostic\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The other observations Prieve repeatedly dismisses as "not consistent with hail" are dents that also include scrape marks (e.g., A-45, A-64, A-107, A-113, A-115\'96117, A-156, A-159, A-166). The "smooth or rounded" diagnostic implies that a hail dent should be free of scrape marks. But there is no peer-reviewed support for that proposition in his report \'97 and the HailShapes literature in your project folder establishes the opposite: real hailstones are rarely perfectly smooth or spherical, often have lobes, points, and irregular surfaces, and routinely produce impact signatures that include abrasions and scrape marks (especially in oblique-angle impacts driven by wind, like the 48 MPH gust the NWS recorded at Eden Prairie ASOS at 1:00 AM on 7/14/2024).\
So Prieve's two-track diagnostic \'97 (i) smooth/rounded = hail, but only if the 2020 file already covered it, and (ii) scrape marks inside dents = blunt object other than hail \'97 is constructed to make every observation a non-hail finding regardless of what is actually present. That's a tell of advocacy, not forensic methodology.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 3. It strengthens the 
\f3\i Quade
\f1\i0  preclusion / appraisal-award argument\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 The 9/12/2025 appraisal panel awarded F1.7 (a single-sash window at the corner of stairs facing front) and B1.2 (a three-sash window in family room facing back). At least one of those \'97 the front/west triple window \'97 is where Prieve documented 
\f1\b "rounded dents"
\f0\b0  in his own narrative. The panel's award of hail causation on that window aligns with Prieve's own diagnostic, not against it. Prieve's report therefore does not just lose to the appraisal panel \'97 it tacitly agrees with the panel's causation finding while reaching the opposite conclusion. That's strong material for the 
\f4\i Quade
\f0\i0  binding-effect argument and for the post-award "Farmers can't consistently describe its own scope" argument.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 4. It gives the Prieve deposition a clean, narrow opening\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This is the textbook locked-in admission for cross-examination. The sequence:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Read aloud the garage-window sentence: "No smooth or rounded dents were observed in the frame or sashes."\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Confirm that "smooth or rounded" is the characteristic Prieve was looking for at the garage window to identify hail damage.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Confirm that the standard applied at the garage window is the same standard Prieve applied elsewhere in the report.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Walk him to page 4 of his report: "Rounded dents were observed at sashes of this window unit." (Front/west triple window.) Confirm.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Walk him to page 5: "Two dents appear at the lower left corner of the left casement which are generally smooth." (North porch window.) Confirm.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	6	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ask: by your own diagnostic, what causes "rounded" and "generally smooth" dents on these windows?\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	7	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 He will either (a) say "hail" \'97 which directly defeats Conclusion 1's "no recent hail damage has occurred" \'97 or (b) say "the 2020 storm." If (b), ask why the 2020 storm produced smooth/rounded dents but the 7/13/2024 storm \'97 with hail documented by NWS at up to 3.5\'964 inches in the broader event \'97 did not.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 There is no escape. This is the kind of sentence a Daubert motion is built around.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 5. It gives you a clean Request for Admission for Tranche A\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 "Prieve Engineering report (File #241008) treats the presence of 'smooth or rounded' dents as a diagnostic indicator of hail-caused damage to window frames and sashes."\
A categorical denial is impeachable by the garage-window sentence; an admission locks Farmers into the diagnostic and then loses the rest of the report on application.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 6. It exposes the cherry-picking of the "scrape mark" framing\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Prieve uses "scrape mark inside dent" at least seven times on a single page (the east-side narrative) as the reason to call observations "not consistent with hail." But "scrape mark" is never defined in the report; it has no documented size, depth, geometry, or material threshold; and Prieve's only stated affirmative criterion for hail \'97 implicit at the garage window \'97 is "smooth or rounded." So the operative framework is: dents I want to attribute to hail are "rounded"; dents I want to attribute to non-hail are "scrape marks inside dents." That is conclusion-first analysis.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
